LM.SEARCH_INDEX = {"version":1,"records":[{"id":"page:a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation","group":"pages","title":"Alphabet, pronunciation and stress","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Spanish is read the way it is written. Learn the few letters that differ from English, where the stress falls, and ¿ ¡.","text":"Spanish spelling is regular: once you know the letters, you can read any word aloud and usually write a word you hear. Compare English though, through and tough. Spanish has five clear vowels, a few letters that differ from English, and simple stress rules. How it works Vowels. There are five, and each has one short, clear sound. They never change into \"uh\" as in English banana. Letter Sounds like Example a a in father casa (house) e e in bed mesa (table) i ee in see, but short sí (yes) o o in go, but short, without the w glide at the end ocho (eight) u oo in food, short uno (one) Letters that differ from English. Letter How to say it Example h silent hola, hotel j, and g before e / i a breathy sound from the throat, like a strong h José, gente ñ ny as in canyon España, mañana ll, y y as in yes (in some regions closer to j in jam; in Argentina and Uruguay like sh) calle, yo r between vowels one quick tap, like tt in American better pero (but) rr, and r at the start of a word or after n, l, s a rolled r perro (dog), rojo, Enrique b, v the same sound vaso, bar c before e / i, z th in think in most of Spain; s in Latin America, the Canary Islands and parts of southern Spain cine, zona c before a / o / u, g before a / o / u k; g in go casa, gracias qu k (the u is silent) queso Stress. Every word has one strong syllable. Three rules find it: A word ending in a vowel, n or s is stressed on the second-to-last syllable: CA-sa, HA-blan, LU-nes. A word ending in any other consonant is stressed on the last syllable: ho-TEL, ciu-DAD. A written accent breaks the rules and shows the stressed syllable: ca-FÉ, te-LÉ-fo-no, Jo-SÉ. Questions and exclamations have a mark at the start, upside down: ¿Cómo te llamas? ¡Hola! When to use it Reading a new word. Say each letter, then put the stress where the rules say. Spelling your name. People will ask ¿Cómo se escribe? (How do you spell it?). The letter names: a, be, ce, de, e, efe, ge, hache, i, jota, ka, ele, eme, ene, eñe, o, pe, cu, erre, ese, te, u, uve (Spain) / ve (Latin America), uve doble (Spain) / doble ve or doble u (Latin America), equis, ye, zeta. Writing. A missing accent or ñ can change the meaning: papa (potato, Latin America) and papá (dad); ano (anus) and año (year). Examples Spanish English hola hello (the h is silent) España Spain la calle the street el perro the dog (rolled rr) pero but (one tap) el teléfono the telephone (te-LÉ-fo-no) ¿Cómo te llamas? What's your name? Good to know ch sounds as in church: chocolate. Common mistakes ✗ Como te llamas? → ✓ ¿Cómo te llamas? — a question needs ¿ at the start, and question words have an accent. ✗ Espana → ✓ España — n and ñ are two different letters. ✗ pero (for \"dog\") → ✓ perro — one r is a tap; rr is a roll, and it is a different word. ✗ Jose → ✓ José — the name is stressed on the last syllable, and the accent shows it. Practice Where is the stress? Write the stressed syllable. mesa hotel café hablan Write the word correctly. Espana (Spain) el pero (the dog) Como te llamas? (What's your name?) ola (hello) Answers ME-sa (ends in a vowel) ho-TEL (ends in l) ca-FÉ (written accent) HA-blan (ends in n) España el perro ¿Cómo te llamas? hola","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-alphabet-pronunciation"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-greetings-introductions","group":"pages","title":"Greet people, say goodbye and introduce yourself","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Say hello at any time of day, give your name, ask someone's name, react politely and say goodbye.","text":"These are the first sentences you will use, and you can learn them as fixed phrases before you know the grammar inside them. One difference from English matters from day one: Spanish has two ways to say \"you\". Use tú with friends, family, children and people your age; use usted with older people, strangers in formal situations, and customers or senior people at work. Many phrases below come in both versions. Key phrases Saying hello Spanish English Hola. Hi. / Hello. (any time, any person) Buenos días. Good morning. (until noon in Latin America; until lunch, about 2 p.m., in Spain) Buenas tardes. Good afternoon. / Good evening. (until it gets dark) Buenas noches. Good evening. / Good night. (after dark, also to say goodbye) ¿Qué tal? How's it going? (very common, friendly) ¿Cómo estás? / ¿Cómo está usted? How are you? (informal / formal) Bien, gracias. ¿Y tú? / ¿Y usted? Fine, thanks. And you? Introducing yourself and others Spanish English Me llamo Ana. My name is Ana. Soy Ana. I'm Ana. ¿Cómo te llamas? What's your name? (informal) ¿Cómo se llama usted? What's your name? (formal) Este es Pedro. / Esta es Lucía. This is Pedro. / This is Lucía. Mucho gusto. Nice to meet you. Encantado. / Encantada. Pleased to meet you. (a man says encantado, a woman encantada) Igualmente. Likewise. Saying goodbye Spanish English Adiós. Goodbye. Hasta luego. See you later. (also for \"bye\" in a shop) Hasta mañana. See you tomorrow. Nos vemos. See you. (informal) Chao. Bye. (informal, common in Latin America) Dialogue At a language exchange, two people meet. Marta: ¡Hola! Me llamo Marta. ¿Y tú? (Hi! My name is Marta. And you?) Tom: Hola, Marta. Soy Tom. (Hi, Marta. I'm Tom.) Marta: Mucho gusto, Tom. (Nice to meet you, Tom.) Tom: Igualmente. ¿Qué tal? (Likewise. How's it going?) Marta: Muy bien, gracias. Mira, esta es Elena. (Very well, thanks. Look, this is Elena.) Elena: Hola, Tom. Encantada. (Hi, Tom. Pleased to meet you.) Tom: Encantado, Elena. (Pleased to meet you, Elena.) Marta: Bueno, hasta luego. (OK, see you later.) Tom and Elena: ¡Hasta luego! (See you later!) Culture note In Spain friends and new acquaintances often greet with two kisses, one on each cheek; in much of Latin America one kiss is usual. Men usually shake hands with each other. Buenas on its own is a friendly short form of buenos días or buenas tardes. When in doubt, start with usted. People will tell you if they prefer tú: Puedes tutearme (\"you can say tú to me\"). Common mistakes ✗ Buenos tardes. → ✓ Buenas tardes. — tarde and noche are feminine words, so it is buenas; only días takes buenos. ✗ Me llamo es Ana. → ✓ Me llamo Ana. — me llamo already means \"my name is\"; there is no extra verb. ✗ Encantado (said by a woman) → ✓ Encantada — the word agrees with the person speaking. ✗ ¿Cómo te llamas? (to an older stranger at an office) → ✓ ¿Cómo se llama usted? — use the formal version with people you would call \"sir\" or \"madam\". ✗ Bien, y tú? → ✓ Bien, ¿y tú? — a question inside a sentence still opens with ¿. Practice Choose the right greeting. It is 9 a.m. You walk into a bakery: (Buenos días / Buenas noches) It is 4 p.m.: (Buenas tardes / Buenos tardes) Complete the dialogue. —Hola, me Carlos. —Mucho gusto. Yo soy Rosa. —Encantada. —. (Likewise.) —¿Qué tal? —Bien, gracias. ¿Y ? (to a friend) Translate into Spanish. What's your name? (to your teacher's grandmother) See you tomorrow! Pleased to meet you. (said by a woman) Answers Buenos días Buenas tardes llamo Igualmente tú ¿Cómo se llama usted? / ¿Cómo se llama? ¡Hasta mañana! Encantada. / Mucho gusto.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-greetings-introductions"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-subject-pronouns","group":"pages","title":"Subject pronouns; tú vs usted","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"I, you, he, she, we, they in Spanish; four words for \"you\"; and why Spanish often leaves the pronoun out.","text":"Subject pronouns are the words for \"I\", \"you\", \"she\" and so on. Spanish has more of them than English, for two reasons. First, \"you\" has a friendly form and a polite form, and a singular and a plural. Second, \"we\" and \"they\" show whether the group is all female. But Spanish uses them much less, because the verb ending already shows who is meant. How it works English Spanish Notes I yo no capital letter you (one friend) tú with an accent; tu without it means \"your\" he / she él / ella él with an accent; el without it means \"the\" you (one person, formal) usted often written Ud. we nosotros / nosotras nosotras: only women you (a group of friends) (Spain) vosotros / vosotras vosotras: only women they ellos / ellas ellas: only women you (a group) ustedes formal in Spain; all groups in Latin America. Often Uds. A mixed group takes the masculine form: Ana and Pedro are ellos; Ana, Rosa and Pedro are still ellos. Usted and ustedes mean \"you\", but they take the same verb form as él and ellos: ¿Cómo está usted? is \"How are you?\" There is no word for \"it\" as a subject: Es grande. (It is big.) When to use it Choose tú or usted. Tú for friends, family, children and people your age in relaxed settings. Usted for older people, customers, officials and anyone you would call \"sir\" or \"madam\". To a friend: ¿Y tú? To your new boss: ¿Y usted? Talk to a group. In Spain, vosotros for friends and ustedes for formal groups. In Latin America, ustedes for every group. Both are correct; use the one people use where you are. Leave the pronoun out. In a normal sentence the verb is enough: Me llamo Ana (not Yo me llamo Ana every time). Use the pronoun for contrast or emphasis. Yo me llamo Ana y él se llama Pedro. (I'm Ana, and he is Pedro.) —¿Quién es Marta? —Yo. (Me.) Examples Spanish English —Bien, gracias. ¿Y tú? Fine, thanks. And you? —Muy bien. ¿Y usted, señora? Very well. And you, madam? Él se llama Luis y ella se llama Rosa. He's called Luis and she's called Rosa. ¿Cómo se llama usted? What's your name? (formal) —¿Y vosotros? —Nosotros, bien. (Spain) \"And you guys?\" \"We're fine.\" —¿Y ustedes? —Nosotras, muy bien. \"And you?\" \"We're very well.\" (a group of women) Mucho gusto. Yo soy Pablo. Nice to meet you. I'm Pablo. Good to know In Argentina, Uruguay, Paraguay and much of Central America (and in parts of other countries) people say vos instead of tú with different verb forms (vos sos, vos tenés). You will understand it from context; this course uses tú. Common mistakes ✗ Yo me llamo Ana. Yo soy de Lima. Yo soy profesora. → ✓ Me llamo Ana. Soy de Lima. Soy profesora. — repeating yo in every sentence sounds unnatural. ✗ Usted te llamas Pedro. → ✓ Usted se llama Pedro. — usted takes the forms of él, not of tú. ✗ Ellos (for Ana and María) → ✓ Ellas — a group of only women is ellas, and nosotras for \"we\". ✗ Él es grande. (about a table) → ✓ Es grande. — there is no subject pronoun for \"it\". ✗ tu (for \"you\") → ✓ tú — without the accent, tu means \"your\". Practice Which pronoun? Write it in Spanish. you, to your best friend you, to an old man you meet in a museum they (Elena and Sofía) we (you and your brother; you are a woman) Translate into Spanish. And you? (to a group of friends in Mexico) And you? (to a group of friends in Madrid) He's called Pablo. What's your name? (to a hotel receptionist) Answers tú usted ellas nosotros ¿Y ustedes? ¿Y vosotros? / ¿Y vosotras? (¿Y ustedes? is also understood) Se llama Pablo. / Él se llama Pablo. ¿Cómo se llama usted? / ¿Cómo se llama?","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-subject-pronouns"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-ser","group":"pages","title":"ser: identity, origin and profession","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The first \"to be\": say who you are, where you are from, what you do and what someone is like.","text":"English has one verb \"to be\". Spanish has two, ser and estar, and they are not interchangeable. This page is about ser, the verb for what something is: a name, a nationality, a job, a lasting quality. You will meet estar (where something is, how someone feels) in unit 3. Ser is irregular, so learn its six forms by heart. How it works Person ser English yo soy I am tú eres you are él, ella, usted es he / she is, you are (formal) nosotros, nosotras somos we are vosotros, vosotras (Spain) sois you are (plural) ellos, ellas, ustedes son they are, you are (plural) Negative: put no before the verb: No soy de aquí. (I'm not from here.) Question: the same words, with ¿ ? and rising intonation: ¿Eres de México? (Are you from Mexico?) Origin: ser + de + place: Soy de Chile. Jobs: no un / una before the job: Es médico. (He is a doctor.) When to use it Name and identity. Soy Marta. (I'm Marta.) Es mi amigo. (He is my friend.) Origin and nationality. Somos de Colombia. (We're from Colombia.) Es español. (He is Spanish.) Nationalities are written with a small letter. Job. Soy profesor. (I'm a teacher; a woman says profesora.) Son estudiantes. (They are students.) What someone or something is like (a lasting quality). Es inteligente. (She is smart.) Eres muy amable. (You are very kind.) Day and date. Hoy es lunes. (Today is Monday.) Examples Spanish English Soy Daniel. Soy de Canadá. I'm Daniel. I'm from Canada. ¿Eres estudiante? Are you a student? Mi madre es médica. My mother is a doctor. No somos de aquí. We're not from here. ¿De dónde es usted? Where are you from? (formal) Estos son Ana y Luis. Son de Madrid. This is Ana and Luis. They're from Madrid. Mi profesora es muy simpática. My teacher is very nice. ¿Sois hermanos? (Spain) Are you brothers? / Are you brother and sister? Good to know Es can mean \"he is\", \"she is\", \"it is\" and \"you are\" (formal). The context tells you which; add él, ella or usted only if it is not clear. With a job plus a description, the article comes back: Es un médico muy bueno. (He is a very good doctor.) For age Spanish uses tener, not ser: Tengo 25 años. See . Common mistakes ✗ Soy un profesor. → ✓ Soy profesor. — no un / una before a job on its own. ✗ Soy 20 años. → ✓ Tengo 20 años. — age is \"having years\" in Spanish. ✗ Yo es de Chile. → ✓ Yo soy de Chile. — each person has its own form: yo soy, él es. ✗ Es en Madrid. (where a person is) → ✓ Está en Madrid. — location of people and things takes estar; ser de is for origin (events are different; you will see them later). Practice Complete with the right form of ser. Yo de Perú. ¿Tú profesora? Nosotros amigos. Ellas de Italia. Usted muy amable. Translate into Spanish. I'm not from here. She is a nurse. Where are you from? (to a friend) Answers soy eres somos son es No soy de aquí. Es enfermera. / Ella es enfermera. ¿De dónde eres? / ¿De dónde eres tú?","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-ser"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-numbers-days-months","group":"pages","title":"Numbers 0–100, days of the week, months","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Count to one hundred, name the days and months, and say \"on Monday\" and \"in July\" the Spanish way.","text":"Numbers, days and months are everywhere: prices, phone numbers, plans. Two things surprise English speakers. Days and months are written with a small letter in Spanish. And you say \"on Monday\" without any word for \"on\": just el lunes. Words Numbers 0–20 Spanish English cero, uno, dos, tres, cuatro, cinco 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 seis, siete, ocho, nueve, diez 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 once, doce, trece, catorce, quince 11, 12, 13, 14, 15 dieciséis, diecisiete, dieciocho, diecinueve, veinte 16, 17, 18, 19, 20 Numbers 21–100 Spanish English veintiuno, veintidós, veintitrés 21, 22, 23 veinticuatro … veintinueve 24 … 29 treinta, treinta y uno, treinta y dos 30, 31, 32 cuarenta, cincuenta, sesenta 40, 50, 60 setenta, ochenta, noventa 70, 80, 90 noventa y nueve 99 cien 100 16–29 are one word: diecisiete, veintiocho. From 31 they are three words with y: cuarenta y cinco. Watch the accents: dieciséis, veintidós, veintitrés, veintiséis. Uno becomes un before a masculine noun and una before a feminine one: un euro, una persona, veintiún años. Days of the week (the week starts on Monday) Spanish English el lunes Monday el martes Tuesday el miércoles Wednesday el jueves Thursday el viernes Friday el sábado Saturday el domingo Sunday el fin de semana the weekend Months Spanish English enero, febrero, marzo January, February, March abril, mayo, junio April, May, June julio, agosto, septiembre July, August, September octubre, noviembre, diciembre October, November, December Word partners Spanish English Hoy es martes. Today is Tuesday. el lunes on Monday (this coming Monday) los lunes on Mondays (every Monday) de lunes a viernes from Monday to Friday el fin de semana on the weekend en julio in July en verano in summer el número de teléfono the phone number Mi número es seis, cinco, cero… My number is six, five, zero… ¿Cuánto es? —Son veinte euros. How much is it? —It's twenty euros. Good to know Phone numbers are read digit by digit or in pairs: 61 20 34 → sesenta y uno, veinte, treinta y cuatro. Septiembre is also spelled setiembre in some countries. Days ending in -s do not change in the plural: el lunes, los lunes. Sábado and domingo add -s: los sábados. Common mistakes ✗ Hoy es Lunes. → ✓ Hoy es lunes. — days and months take a small letter. ✗ en el lunes → ✓ el lunes — Spanish has no word for \"on\" with days. ✗ veinte y dos → ✓ veintidós — today 21–29 are written as one word. ✗ en Julio → ✓ en julio — months also take a small letter. Practice Write the number in words. 15 22 48 100 Translate into Spanish. on Saturday on Fridays in October Today is Wednesday. Answers quince veintidós cuarenta y ocho cien el sábado los viernes en octubre Hoy es miércoles.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-numbers-days-months"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-gustar","group":"pages","title":"gustar and similar verbs","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Say what you like and don't like. In Spanish the thing you like is the subject, not you.","text":"In English you say \"I like coffee\", and I is the subject. Spanish turns the sentence around: Me gusta el café is, word for word, \"Coffee pleases me\". The thing you like is the subject; you are the person it pleases. That is why you only need two forms of the verb: gusta and gustan. How it works (a + person) + pronoun + gusta / gustan + what you like Who likes it Pronoun One thing, or an action Several things (a mí) me gusta gustan (a ti) te gusta gustan (a él, a ella, a usted) le gusta gustan (a nosotros, a nosotras) nos gusta gustan (a vosotros, a vosotras) (Spain) os gusta gustan (a ellos, a ellas, a ustedes) les gusta gustan gusta + singular noun with its article: Me gusta el café. gusta + infinitive, also with two or more infinitives: Me gusta bailar. Me gusta cantar y bailar. gustan + plural noun: Me gustan los gatos. Negative: no goes before the pronoun: No me gusta el té. Questions keep the same word order: ¿Te gusta el fútbol? When to use it Say what you like or don't like. Me gusta la música. (I like music.) No me gustan los lunes. (I don't like Mondays.) Ask someone. To a friend: ¿Te gusta cocinar? Formal: ¿Le gusta la habitación? Say how much. Me gusta mucho el cine. (I like movies a lot.) No me gusta nada el frío. (I don't like the cold at all.) Me encanta means \"I love\": Me encanta esta ciudad. Make clear who. Le can mean he, she or you (formal), so add a + the person: A María le gusta el cine. A mí no. Agree or disagree. —Me gusta el café. —A mí también. (Me too.) —No me gusta el té. —A mí tampoco. (Me neither.) Examples Spanish English Me gusta el chocolate. I like chocolate. ¿Te gustan los perros? Do you like dogs? No nos gusta trabajar los sábados. We don't like working on Saturdays. A Carlos le gusta mucho viajar. Carlos really likes traveling. A mis padres les gustan las películas antiguas. My parents like old movies. Me encanta esta ciudad. I love this city. ¿Os gusta la comida mexicana? (Spain) Do you all like Mexican food? —No me gusta el frío. —A mí tampoco. \"I don't like the cold.\" \"Me neither.\" Good to know The same pattern works for encantar (to love), interesar (to interest) and doler (to hurt): Me interesan los idiomas. Me duele la cabeza. Me encanta is already strong. Do not add mucho. About people, Me gustas means \"I am attracted to you\". To say you like someone as a person, use Me cae bien: Tu hermano me cae muy bien. Common mistakes ✗ Yo gusto el café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. — you are not the subject; the coffee is. ✗ Me gusta los gatos. → ✓ Me gustan los gatos. — the verb agrees with the thing you like. ✗ Me gusta café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. — things you like in general need the article. ✗ María le gusta bailar. → ✓ A María le gusta bailar. — the person needs a. ✗ Me gusto bailar. → ✓ Me gusta bailar. — me gusto means \"I like myself\". Practice Complete with the pronoun and gusta or gustan. (a mí) la paella. (a ti) ¿ los deportes? A Ana leer. (a nosotros) No las verduras. A mis amigos cantar y bailar. Translate into Spanish. I don't like coffee. Do you like the hotel? (formal) \"I love dogs.\" \"Me too.\" Answers Me gusta Te gustan le gusta nos gustan les gusta No me gusta el café. ¿Le gusta el hotel? —Me encantan los perros. —A mí también.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-gustar"},{"id":"review","group":"exercises","title":"Flashcards and mistakes","headings":[],"summary":"One review queue: deck cards, My mistakes and weak spots from exercises.","text":"flashcards review decks mistakes weak spots","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review"},{"id":"review:words","group":"exercises","title":"My words","headings":[],"summary":"Words and phrases you selected in texts, with their sentence and meaning.","text":"my words personal vocabulary flashcards","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/words"},{"id":"deck:vocab-a1-food-shopping","group":"exercises","title":"Food, drink and shopping (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Sample of 8 cards. The full deck has 30–40 cards for the topic a1-vocabulary-food-shopping.","text":"flashcards deck Food, drink and shopping A1","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/vocab-a1-food-shopping"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-1","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: choose the form, fill the gaps, build sentences and translate.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-2","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: write it yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: type the missing word, rewrite sentences, write what you hear and build a question.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-2"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-3","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: agree, disagree and say how much","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: listen and write, rewrite, find the mistake, match reactions and fill a table with encantar.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-3"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation-1","group":"exercises","title":"Sounds and spelling: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: stress, special letters, accents and question marks.","text":"Topic: Alphabet, pronunciation and stress","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-subject-pronouns-1","group":"exercises","title":"Subject pronouns: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the right pronoun, tú or usted, and when to leave the pronoun out.","text":"Topic: Subject pronouns; tú vs usted","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-subject-pronouns-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-ser-1","group":"exercises","title":"ser: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the forms of ser, origin, jobs and descriptions.","text":"Topic: ser: identity, origin and profession","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-ser-1"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u1","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Sounds and first words","headings":[],"summary":"Sounds and spelling, subject pronouns and ser, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Alphabet, pronunciation and stress, Subject pronouns; tú vs usted, ser: identity, origin and profession","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u1"},{"id":"lab:presente","group":"lab","title":"Presente","headings":["Present"],"summary":"What is true now, what you do regularly, and general facts.","text":"True now Vivo en un apartamento pequeño cerca del centro. I live in a small apartment near downtown. Habits and routines Los sábados juego al tenis con mi hermana. On Saturdays I play tennis with my sister. General facts El sol sale por el este. The sun rises in the east. Happening at the moment ¿Qué haces? —Leo una novela. What are you doing? —I'm reading a novel. Fixed plans and timetables Mañana trabajo hasta las ocho. Tomorrow I'm working until eight. siempre normalmente todos los días los lunes a veces nunca ahora English says “I'm reading” for an action going on now. Spanish can just use the present: Leo una novela. Estoy leyendo is also very common, but you do not need it every time. The ending already shows the person, so the subject pronoun is usually left out: Vivo en Lima. Using yo in every sentence sounds unnatural; keep it for emphasis or contrast. Stem changes do not happen in the nosotros and vosotros forms: queremos, podemos, not *quieremos, *puedemos.","level":"A1","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/presente"},{"id":"lab:preterito-indefinido","group":"lab","title":"Pretérito indefinido","headings":["Preterite"],"summary":"Finished actions at a definite time in the past.","text":"One finished action Ayer comí con Lucía. Yesterday I had lunch with Lucía. A chain of actions Llegué a casa, cené y me acosté. I got home, had dinner and went to bed. A closed period of time Viví tres años en Lima. I lived in Lima for three years. ayer anoche el lunes la semana pasada en 2019 hace dos años The English simple past covers both one finished event and a habit: “I ate there yesterday” / “As a kid I ate there every Sunday”. Spanish separates them: comí (once, finished) and comía (habit or background). Present yo and preterite él differ only by the accent: hablo (I speak) / habló (he or she spoke). The preterite yo form is hablé.","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/preterito-indefinido"},{"id":"lab:preterito-imperfecto","group":"lab","title":"Pretérito imperfecto","headings":["Imperfect"],"summary":"Descriptions, background and habits in the past.","text":"Habits in the past De niña vivía en un pueblo e iba a la escuela a pie. As a girl I lived in a village and walked to school. Descriptions La casa era vieja y tenía un jardín enorme. The house was old and had a huge garden. Background to an event Hacía frío y llovía cuando llegamos. It was cold and raining when we arrived. Time and how someone felt Eran las once y yo tenía mucho sueño. It was eleven o'clock and I was very sleepy. antes de niño / de niña siempre todos los veranos normalmente mientras cuando era joven English “used to play” and “was playing” are both often jugaba in Spanish. The imperfecto covers habits and actions in progress. The imperfecto does not tell you that an action finished. For one finished event at a definite time, use the indefinido: Ayer vi a Pablo. Ayer veía a Pablo sounds unfinished, as if the story goes on. Watch the accents: hablábamos (only the nosotros form of -ar verbs), but every -er and -ir form has í: comía, vivíamos.","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/preterito-imperfecto"},{"id":"lab:ir-a-infinitivo","group":"lab","title":"ir a + infinitivo","headings":["going to + infinitive"],"summary":"Plans, intentions and things that are clearly about to happen.","text":"Plans Este verano vamos a viajar a Perú. This summer we're going to travel to Peru. Intentions El próximo año voy a estudiar más. Next year I'm going to study more. About to happen (you can see it) ¡Cuidado, te vas a caer! Careful, you're going to fall! Let's… (vamos a) Bueno, vamos a empezar. OK, let's start. mañana esta noche este fin de semana el próximo año la semana que viene pronto Do not leave out a: Voy a comer, not *Voy comer. After a comes the infinitive, never a conjugated verb: Vamos a salir, not *Vamos a salimos. English “I'm going to the movies” has no second verb. In Spanish that is ir a + a place: Voy al cine.","level":"A1","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/ir-a-infinitivo"},{"id":"lab:ser-estar","group":"lab","title":"ser vs estar","headings":["what someone or something is","where it is and how it is now"],"summary":"Two verbs for “to be”: ser says what something is, estar says where it is and how it is right now.","text":"Identity and jobs Who or what someone is, including their job. Origin Where someone or something comes from. What it is like Lasting characteristics: personality, looks, size, color. Events Where or when an event happens: a party, a class, a concert. Time and dates The time, the day and the date. Location Where a person or a thing is, even when it never moves. (Where an event happens takes ser.) Feelings and states How someone feels or what state something is in at the moment. bien and mal Asking and saying how someone is. *Soy cansado → Estoy cansado. Feelings and states take estar. *Está médico → Es médico. Jobs take ser. *La farmacia es en esta calle → La farmacia está en esta calle. Location takes estar. *Soy 20 años → Tengo 20 años. Age takes tener, not ser or estar.","level":"A1","category":"Contrast card","route":"#/lab/contrast/ser-estar"},{"id":"lab-tree:ser-or-estar","group":"lab","title":"ser or estar?","headings":[],"summary":"Think of the sentence you want to say with “to be”. A few questions lead to the right verb, and sometimes it is neither of the two.","text":"What does “to be” tell us in your sentence? Where a person or a thing is estar Where an event happens: a party, a class, a concert ser Who someone is, their job, or where they are from ser The time, the day or the date ser How old someone is tener What someone or something is like, or how they are  Is it what they are like in general, or how they are at the moment? What they are like in general: personality, looks, size, color ser How they feel or how they are right now: tired, glad, ill, busy estar Just “How are you?” or “I'm fine” estar","level":"A1","category":"Decision tree","route":"#/lab/pick/ser-or-estar"}]};
